  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 AM EDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nAnother day of well above normal temperatures is in store as some \nWAA continues. Remnants of \"Isaias\" will continue slowly north \nthrough the day and eventually more northeast tonight into Sunday. \nBands of cirrus will work across the area, generally thin in nature \nuntil the circulation edges closer. Scattered showers may quickly \nmove north across much of the area near/after 3Z Sun then steadier \nprecip arriving after 6Z and lingering along and east of Interstate \n69. QPF is somewhat lower than past days with amounts possibly at \nmost less than one half inch. Model blends still keeping some slgt \nchc pops for thunder. Lapse rates not overly impressive, but some \nelevated instability is noted (negative Showalters). Will leave in \nfor the time being. \n\nWith regards to temps on Sunday, some guidance suggests high temps \ncould be several degrees cooler (mainly east) which could have some \nvalidity with the extensive cloud cover and precip. These temps seem \nto be in the lower percentile range, so have left previous highs \nalone and pass onto the next shift. \n\nLull in precip chances arrives Sunday night into Monday, before a \nseries of weak waves and fronts arrive for mid week. Thereafter, med \nrange models have sort of diverged some on the degree of phasing of \na weaker northern and stronger southern stream wave. Pops remain in \nplace with signs still pointing towards cooling for the weekend, but \nthe degree of cooling and amount of precip is in question with these \nvariations.",
